      "title": "JEE Advanced 2023 (Paper 2)"
    }
  ],
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Consider the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>6</mn></math>&nbsp;square in the figure. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>49</mn></msub></math> be the points of intersections (dots in the picture) in some order. We say that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></math> are friends if they are adjacent along a row or along a column. Assume that each point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> has an equal chance of being chosen.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/d33e0b30-a00c-486d-8560-c6e3e5f7757d.png\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 197px;\" /></p>\n\n<p>(There are two questions based on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>PARAGRAPH</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#34;</mo><mi>II</mi><mo>&#34;</mo></math>, the question given below is one of them)</p>\n<br>Two distinct points are chosen randomly out of the points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>49</mn></msub></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi></math>&nbsp;be the probability that they are friends. Then the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>p</mi></math> is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "0.5",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Given,</p><p>We have to choose two distinct points randomly,</p><p>Now number of ways of selecting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> adjacent dots in row will be<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math>&nbsp;{as if you pick any dot then you have pick second dot from remaining <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math>&nbsp;dots, so total ways will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn><none></none><mprescripts></mprescripts><none></none><mn>6</mn></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math>}</p><p>Similarly, number of ways of selecting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> adjacent dots in&nbsp;column<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo></math>Number of ways of selecting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> adjacent dots from the given diagram we will&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>84</mn></math></p><p>So, probability is given by,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>84</mn><mmultiscripts><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn><none></none><mprescripts></mprescripts><none></none><mn>49</mn></mmultiscripts></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>84</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>49</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>84</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>49</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p></blockquote>",
    "image": null
  },
